{
  "dom_id": "question-127106",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$)$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nEarlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+12 & >0 \\\\ -12x & >-12 \\\\ x & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,1)$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
